<b>Interview 10: &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;: architect, male, born in 1970s, Nanjing, Jiangsu</b>

<p><b> Is it your first time to visit a foreign country?</b>
<p>It is not my first time abroad. I have been in Germany and some other European countries, and Japan too. I find it difficult to generalize all other countries into the simple term "foreign country", and thus I find it hard to tell what has impressed me the most, or maybe there are too many impressive things to place them in order by exclamation point.
<p>I would not mind talking about Kassel. As to a tourist, such a small city, to stay a week is very long. The city leaves me with an imprssion of peace and order. Even the parade that day seemed so, orderly,  rhythmic drums and slogans, as if it had been well rehearsed. As to the art exhibition, except for a very few exhibits, all the rest has been blurred in my mind. One reason is that I was rather careless in the exhibition, and the other is that not many have really aroused my interest. it is said that the city had been in debris after WWII, so its reconstruction has been at most 60 years.  I would like to know the process of its development.

<p><b>In your point of view, what are the similiarities and differences between Chinese and Western people?</b>
<p>Chinese and Western people? It is difficult to conceptualize the two categories.  At the very least, Should we instead, refer to them as Eastern and Western people, or Chinese and German? Does Chinese culture mean Han culture or the culture in which the Han culture is the majority? It is difficult to define western culture. There exists huge differences just between the Arabic and Greek cultures. I might be picky, haha, forgive me, please. Indistinctly I understand your question, and I think that peoples in the world differ from each other greatly and slightly.

<p><b>Do you have any faith?</b>
<p>We have all grown up under the red flag, and who does not believe in Communism? But in today's age, can you ask how many people still believe in Communism? I don't remember who remarked that: it is unfortunate for one under the age of 20 not to believe in Communism while one is brainless if he or she is over 40 but still believes in Communism. I am around 30 years old, and now in the vaccuum of faith. The faith in Communism is graudally being overthrown by reality, and where is a new faith? It is difficult for me to answer you at this moment, because I am asking myself the same question. To me it is horrible to be without a faith. Frequently I feel frustrated, with the loss of scale, and the sense of nothingness: how shall I spend my life?

<p><b>How much do you understand traditional Chinese culture?</b>
<p>I think that the traditional roots have been planted, but they have not yet bloomed.  I am not sure what constitutes the core value of traditional Chinese culture, the middle way? The loss and maintenance of traditional culture are the concern of those scholars. They are campaigning, being afraid that China will turn European or American. I am not a scholar, and what I see is that we fart Chinese gas even when we have Western food. Just  look at those Chinatowns all over the world. While their taste changes, Chinese restaurants are so tenacious. Needless to mention our own country. Imagine the entire of Westernization? People are daydreaming!

<p><b>What are your everyday concerns?</b>
<p>Recently what I am concerned with is that I have to make some money. Otherwise, how can I cope with when I have children or when my parents get old? Supporting aging parents, education, and medication are all those that I am supposed to share the burden with the Party. Of course I feel anxiety. The issue of survival worries me the most. International issues, and energy are reserved for those who eat a full meal but have little to do.  It is meanless for me to worry about those things. In Germany I came across two Dutch people who are particularly intersted in international issues. They asked a lot about China and Sudan. I think that they need a bit more life pressure, or they will keep caring about other peoples' domestic issues.

<p><b>Do you think about war?</b>
<p>War is like a strong man robbing an apple from a weak man. I do not care about these things a lot, and there is nothing unexpected. I do not believe in any way of justice, while I am sympathic with those who are forced to participate. There is little change in the war areas after so many years. Always those few areas, and always those few apples.
<p>Anything else in addition to their interests between states?  Anything good for all they would do is like bothers, one party would deny to do if it faces some loss, while the party facing a severe loss would fuck the other to death. Germany is a great country, because so many people are doing what Lei Feng did without knowing Lei Feng (a model solider who did good deeds in Mao’s China). With such citizens it is difficult for a country not to be great .

<p><b>Is it urgent to have Sino-Western dialogue?</b>
<p>We began a dialogue with the West when I was in elementary school. States needs long talks. It is good for them to talk, and even the clashing of words, or curses are far better than war. China’s urgent agenda is the opening-up and reform of its government.  Each individual’s future depends on himself and the mysterious destination, but are we really the future of our country?

<p><b>Please share your opinions on art, art critique, conception, and so on.</b>
<p>I am not a male artist, and thus without an artist's life. If anything relates to my life and idea of art, I think, in addition to food, drinking, and fart, I like reading novels, watching pirated discs, listening music, and occasionally writing with Chinese brushes. I used to like painting a lot (very vulgar comics which I thought were hardly any kind of art, but this time I saw them hanging on the exhibit hall at Kassel, under the light of solemnity. ) . I love my profession, and I am an architect. I think it is an art, although many people say not. After careful reflection, I think that it is better to self-deceive. My point of view on art is outdated—beauty. I hate the sick and twisted, but it does not mean that I like those sugar-sweet works.
<p>How much real critique in China? As far as the architecture field is concerned, how much sincere critique is there excluding flattering and irrelevant comments? Nowadays people talk about the so-called “circle” and all that belong to the circle, earning bread in the circle. They meet each other in the morning or night, and have to give face each other. The premise of critique is science and democracy. Without them, critique is nothing but that “you work too hard and thus you do not care about your health.”  Fortunately or not we have advanced “self-critique.”  People analyze themselves before any critique made from their comrades.  Facing such brave and sacrificial figures, how could you criticize them? Are you still Chinese (if you criticize them)?
<p>Although I am not a male artist, and neither have I done any art project, I still think that art is incomparable important. Does this need justification?  The largest reason is that throughout the several thousand years people have kept working on art. What do artists look like? Is he the guy with golden eyes who rides with colorful clouds to marry you? If I am frankly, I would tell you that I like art; If I am damn hypocritical, I would tell you that art is meaningless, and I do not like it, not at all.

<p><b>Do you know any German artists?</b>
<p>What I know includes Ludwig van Beethoven, Wolfgang Amadeus Mozart, Johann Wolfgang von Goethe, Käthe Kollwitz, Bauhaus, Ludwig Mies van der Rohe and so on. I read Grimm's Fairy Tales, and I even remember an original version which is filled with blood, eroticism, and perversion. one section confirmed my long-lasting suspicion that the prince was necrophiliac which is why  he would fall in love with the sleeping beauty. Accuracy and rigorousness but not without romance, can German culture be generalized as this? I am very unsure.  My understanding is fairly limited.

<p><b>How do you perceive love?</b>
<p>I am kind of late-maturing in terms of love. My education since childhood mixes love with sex, parents and grandparents believe that love is not appropriate for children.  So puppy love cannot be exposed. How could love not be important?  Man and woman ( man and man, woman and woman) are more than sexual intercourse. I do not agree that love is the source of art and life. It is the messy things altogether that make our life.
<p>I believe in love. I have not experienced those death-devoted loves, explosive but fragile. Is that love? A friend of mine admires the love in Letter from an Unknown Woman by Stefan Zweig while I was rather terrified of the text. If that is the only definition of love, then I have never had it. Love frequently exist in fairy tales, but in addition to fairy tales, horrible stories occur within love. I remember a moviee "Songs at Midnight" that I watched as a child and scared me to death. I did not realize that it was a love movie until I grew up.   Both bilateral gratification and unrequited love seem to describe love?  In reality unrequited love is much higher.

<p><b>How do you percieve love?  Do you think there are intimate relationships between sex, creative work, and art?</b>
<p>Sex is a normal physiological phenomenon. “Eating, and sex, are our nature.”  Does our country establish a reasonable sexual education? What is a healthy concept of sex? Does it only mean hygiene during sexual intercourse? Based on Marxism we are indoctrinated, things are universally related, so sex, creative work, and art are related, of course. But the degree of intimacy is up to the nature of creative work. To the indulgent and ascetic, sex has a close relationship with their works. But my own experience—is architecture design a kind of creative work?—
<p>I have not designed a project to release my pressure due to a sexual drive, nor have I designed a kitchen or bedroom simply as a space of sexual intercourse. Anyway, it is another matter for resident to use in their own ways.
<p>I had multiple channels to sexual knowledge before my first sexual experience, from erotic magazines to world classic literature, even dictionaries. It is known that when you begin to be interested in sex, even a book of asceticism could be read as erotic. The Chinese attitude to sex might remain the old fashion: simply do it but never speak about it. But now the situation is greatly different. Look at the web pages—watch out! Not erotic websites—you can know how thirsty we are. In a literary way, it might be described as turbulent.

<p><b>How much do you know about environmental protection in China?</b>
<p>I think that what I understand may consistute a tiny tiny portion of the reality. Not because my knowledge is limited, but because the pollution is beyond our imagination. Indeed you may change the question by asking what activities are eco-friendly. The answer is figures that are easily countable. When the water you drink is poisonous, any number of people would sense the crisis. Too many eco-unfriendly things around us including the instant chopsticks we just used. Energy crisis, global warming, and ecological degradation are what is happening around us ordinary folks.  The price of gas keep rising and rising, no more icing pendants in the winter. Running water could be used only to wash cloths. not just to save water and electricityt, but all kinds of energy on a wider scale, including grain.
<p>In Kassel, a buddy of mine and me observed wild ducks with great interest. I exclaimed: I shot at them when I was a child,and now what I can do is to fly to Germany to take a look. I feel that I shall start with myself , and this is better than to join environemntal protection organization formally. Stop wasting can be seen as the first step, otherwise it is pointless. Is it envrionmental protection promotion or moaning? All these sounds have long been engulfed by bigger destrutions. One thing that has not receive any eco-critique suddenly occurs to me: grand movies directed by Zhang Yimou, what a great waste!

<p><b>Does China’s environmental protection mean anything to the world?</b>
<p>Does it mean anything? Is China still located inside Earth?

<p><b>Do you think problems exist in Chinese education?</b>
<p>I may continue the environmental issues mentioned above. Our education is a process in which our body and mind are continously being polluted. A lot of things have to be overthrown when the day of independent thinking comes. And then we begin self-education to wash away those historical marks. So damn un-eco-friendly!  I would never praise my college, never ever. It is sufficient to reveal my open heart as long as I do not curse it.  My five undergraduate years was the period in which I was raped by college education. Now I feel sorry for all of the tuition and other fees my parents saved through their hard life. Indeed massive severe critique to education does not need a word from me. Even so, I still hope that more people can recieve an education, because while being educated risks being polluted, not being educated means being fooled for a longer period of time.

<p><b>Can you talk about your relationship with your parents?</b>
<p>My childhood passed with the strict education of my mom, because my dad was not demobilized from the army until I was six. My mom influenced me more. It is difficult for me to say I was satisfied, but I do not blame them, not at all. Their own limitation led to their education methods. The most memorable is the free life in my rural home village. But I did not like the too high expectations pressed on me, which led to great pressure in my childhood.  My relationship with my parents cannot be expressed by a few sentences.
<p>I am the only child, but I had classmates and neighbors, groups of gangs, so my childhood was not lonely. The most I lacked was  relaxation. My mom was really strict; any naughty activities caused severe results. I won a nickname, “unbeatable Li Kui (Li Kui was a hero in a popular novel Water Margin”, little improvement with many beatings. I had been always the target of strict discipline by teachers, which is why I did not like school.  With being doubly disciplined at home and at school, my grades were ok, but only ok. And teachers’ comments on my behavior remained negative, “the student has very poor self-control ability.”  In school I learned fraternity and resistance against authority. That is one of most important experiences too.

<p><b>Did education influence your life and characteristics deeply?</b>
<p>My last degree was a masters, and it is difficult to evaluate an education degree, maybe not as much as a primary school student, and maybe better than some Ph.Ds. Our education degree is fucking unreliable. Its only usage is to classifiy the salary hierarchy. If you are not working with the system, it would lose its only purpose.  
<p>The influence of education on my life and personality is like a prisoner’s mark on my face, which frequently reminds me of the experience. I tried to erase it, but I could not erase it entirely. This frustrated me often. My education had nothing to do with school and degree, but I cannot deny that people who really educated me were my teachers (as individuals), some being teachers, some classmates, and some friends. Some of their teaching did not benefit me until some years later, and this type of education would never cease. I hope that my children will avoid my education pattern.

<p><b>Do you often feel anxiety?</b>
<p>I do. There are many reasons for anxiety, but sometimes without a reason I begin to worry. It might be because that I am not a carefree. But I do not think I am shallow either. The anxiety may result from the sense of security or the inner controversy of my personality, or from the fact that I am not strong enough. My biggest frustration is that I do not understand myself enough.

<p><b>How do you perceive success and money?</b>
<p>I think that the greatest success comes from one's inner mind. On the day when you no longer hesitate, and you are firm, that is success. I am far away from success, and I have not realized my value. What on earth is my value? I am not sure. But I know, to an architect, value is made.
<p>I once despised money, but that was when I just knew how to spend money and did not know how to make it. In retrospect, I am ashamed of my naïvete. Money is important to everyone, and it is indispensable. However, if we no longer control it, and allow money to drive us, we would be pathetic. When that moment comes, our souls would become despicable, and our actions would be horrible.

<p><b>Do you care about state affairs? Do you think those are related to you?</b>
<p>It’s been a long time that I watched China News Feeds (Xinwen Lianbo) by Chinese Central TV, and as to newspapers, I only read “Southern Weekend”.  Since I am online everyday, I know about state affairs, and I am constantly angry when I hear of injustices. 

<p><b>Do you like your job?</b>
<p>Currently I am an independent architect. It is a refined way to address myself. Another cool name is "gunman." I do not hold a stable position, but my job is architectural design. I like my job; it is like arranging another kind of life, with a wonderful feeling that I cannot describe in words. The largest frustration is that it is a difficult job, and occasionally my brain does not work well. At the same time, it is a collaborative task, so communication barriers exist. Sometime the barriers come from the contractor, a team member, or the construction company, but the job is so pleasant that all these are always overcome, and it difficult to describe with words.  I became sleepless for some time, and only until recently have I felt a bit better.  There are many reasons for insomnia, such as survival pressure, excitement, or obessive-compulsory disorder, and so on. The greatest satisfication that I have obtained from my job comes from my solution to a difficult question, or a meaningful form, so has my sense of accomplishment.
<p>My life is relatively simple and free, a common situation for people who do freelance. I am used to this situation. I find it difficult to evaluate myself. I am still in the period of unrest in youth, and it may become more clear over the years. My wife says that I have a conflicting complex that basically characterizes my personality. There is nothing special in my growth process, so let me not mention it.

